---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO } from '../../data/site';
import envExample from '../../data/env.example?raw';

const toc = [
  ['install', 'Install with Docker Compose'],
  ['configuration', 'Configuration'],
  ['backups', 'Backups and restore'],
  ['upgrading', 'Upgrading'],
  ['reverse-proxy', 'Reverse proxy'],
  ['importing', 'Importing data'],
];
---
<Base
  title="Docs"
  description="Install Axlebook with Docker Compose, configure it, back it up, upgrade it, run it behind Caddy, nginx or Traefik, and import your existing records."
>
  <div class="wrap layout">
    <aside aria-label="On this page">
      <h2 class="toc-title">On this page</h2>
      <ul>{toc.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ul>
    </aside>
    <article class="prose">
      <h1 class="ax-display">Docs</h1>

      <h2 id="install">Install with Docker Compose</h2>
      <p>You need Docker with Compose v2. On Windows and macOS, Docker Desktop works.</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>git clone https://github.com/aubergine-ux/axlebook.git
cd axlebook
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
      <p>
        Open <code>http://localhost:8080</code> and create your account. Everything you enter is kept in the
        <code>axlebook-data</code> volume. To look around first, put <code>SEED_DEMO_DATA=true</code> in a
        <code>.env</code> file next to <code>docker-compose.yml</code> before the first start: you get a 2015
        Tacoma and a 2018 Civic with a couple of years of history.
      </p>
      <h3>Using the published image</h3>
      <p>Images for <code>linux/amd64</code> and <code>linux/arm64</code> are published to GitHub Container Registry with each release.</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`services:
  axlebook:
    image: ghcr.io/aubergine-ux/axlebook:latest
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"
    environment:
      BASE_URL: https://cars.example.com
      TZ: America/Chicago
    volumes:
      - axlebook-data:/data
volumes:
  axlebook-data:`}</code></pre>
      <h3>Postgres instead of SQLite</h3>
      <p>SQLite is plenty for a household. For Postgres, use the bundled overlay or point <code>DATABASE_URL</code> at a server you already run:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pick-something docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.postgres.yml up -d</code></pre>
      <h3>Optional local AI</h3>
      <p>The Ollama overlay adds a private model server; the model downloads once (about 2 GB) on first start:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.ai.yml up -d</code></pre>

      <h2 id="configuration">Configuration</h2>
      <p>
        Everything is an environment variable and everything is optional. Put them in a <code>.env</code> file
        next to <code>docker-compose.yml</code>. This is the app’s
        <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/.env.example`}><code>.env.example</code></a>, exactly as it ships:
      </p>
      <pre class="env" tabindex="0" aria-label=".env.example"><code>{envExample}</code></pre>

      <h2 id="backups">Backups and restore</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>From the app:</strong> Settings, Your data, Download backup. You get a zip with every record,
        reminder, part, inspection, photo and receipt. Admins get the whole instance; everyone else gets the
        vehicles they own.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Restoring:</strong> on a fresh install with no vehicles yet, an admin uses Settings, Your data,
        Restore a backup. Accounts are matched by username. A backup restores into SQLite or Postgres, so this is
        also how you move between them.
      </p>
      <p><strong>From the command line</strong>, for scheduled backups of the data directory:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`docker compose stop axlebook
docker compose cp axlebook:/data ./axlebook-backup-$(date +%F)
docker compose start axlebook`}</code></pre>
      <p>With Postgres, also run <code>pg_dump</code> against the database.</p>

      <h2 id="upgrading">Upgrading</h2>
      <p>Take a backup first. Then, if you built from source:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`git pull
docker compose up -d --build`}</code></pre>
      <p>Or, with the published image:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`docker compose pull
docker compose up -d`}</code></pre>
      <p>The database is upgraded automatically when Axlebook starts. Your data stays in the volume.</p>

      <h2 id="reverse-proxy">Reverse proxy</h2>
      <p>
        Set <code>BASE_URL</code> to the address people type (scheme, host and port all count) and
        <code>TRUST_PROXY=true</code>. Axlebook refuses changes that come from any other origin. Allow uploads at
        least as large as <code>MAX_UPLOAD_MB</code> (25 MB by default).
      </p>
      <h3>Caddy</h3>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`cars.example.com {
	reverse_proxy localhost:8080
}`}</code></pre>
      <h3>nginx</h3>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name cars.example.com;
    # ssl_certificate and ssl_certificate_key go here
    client_max_body_size 25m;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}`}</code></pre>
      <h3>Traefik</h3>
      <p>Labels on the <code>axlebook</code> service, with Traefik’s Docker provider and an entrypoint named <code>websecure</code>:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`services:
  axlebook:
    labels:
      - traefik.enable=true
      - traefik.http.routers.axlebook.rule=Host(\`cars.example.com\`)
      - traefik.http.routers.axlebook.entrypoints=websecure
      - traefik.http.routers.axlebook.tls=true
      - traefik.http.services.axlebook.loadbalancer.server.port=8080
    environment:
      BASE_URL: https://cars.example.com
      TRUST_PROXY: "true"`}</code></pre>

      <h2 id="importing">Importing data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A spreadsheet you already keep:</strong> Garage, Import a spreadsheet. Upload an .xlsx or .csv
          with one sheet per vehicle or several vehicles in one sheet. Check the columns, units and vehicles it
          found, fill in anything missing, preview, then import. Rows already in Axlebook are skipped.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>CSV for one vehicle:</strong> on a vehicle’s Records tab, Import CSV. Choose the record type and date order, preview,
          then import. Axlebook’s own CSV export imports back the same way.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>From LubeLogger (experimental):</strong> export a CSV from LubeLogger and import it the same way;
          its column names are recognised.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Another Axlebook:</strong> restore a backup, as above.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Something didn’t import the way you expected? <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>Open an issue</a> with a sample of
        the file (with personal details removed).
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 24px;
    padding-top: 48px;
  }
  @media (min-width: 960px) {
    .layout {
      grid-template-columns: 220px minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 48px;
    }
    aside ul {
      position: sticky;
      top: 96px;
    }
  }
  .toc-title {
    font-size: 14px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }
  aside ul {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }
  aside li {
    margin: 6px 0;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: 40px;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }
  .env {
    max-height: 640px;
  }
</style>
